# Deliberate binary breaks in 1.14.0, relative to 1.13.0. See the release note in README.md.
#
# THESE FILTERS DO NOT RETIRE. sbt-mima reads every *.backwards.excludes in this directory
# whatever it is named, so this file stays in force until somebody deletes it. Delete it at the
# 1.14.0 post-release, when previousRelease moves to 1.14.0. See #107, where three stale files
# had put 81 filters in force where the build comment implied 36.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All seven breaks below are one change: four methods gain a `(using Random = Random())`.
#
# Every one of them consults adjacency order -- which Gryphon shuffles, by a generator a caller
# is meant to be able to pin with `given Random` (#67) -- and none of them had a parameter for
# that generator to arrive through. So each fell to the `= Random()` default declared on
# `adjacentVertices` / `filteredAdjacencies`, constructing a fresh unseeded generator per vertex
# and discarding it. A caller's seed stopped dead at the call site.
#
# Nothing about the results was wrong. Every topological order returned was a valid topological
# order; every distance computed was the correct distance. What could not be done was reproduce
# a particular run, which is the entire purpose of seeding, and the compiler had been saying so
# all along: `-Wunused` reports `unused local definition` for a `given Random` that nothing
# consumes.
#
# Adding a parameter -- even one with a default -- changes the erased signature, so all four are
# source-compatible and binary-incompatible. A caller recompiling against 1.14.0 needs no edit.
#
# AcyclicShortestPaths is NOT among them, and the reason is worth recording because the obvious
# guess is wrong. It consults adjacency order too, so it looks like a fifth case -- but the only
# order a generator could reach there is inert. `relax` folds over a vertex's outgoing edges and
# each one writes its own key in the predecessor map, so two can compete only by running from one
# vertex to the same target at equal cost: parallel edges, which `fromTriplets` refuses to build
# (#57). Measured on a diamond with two equal-cost paths, forty seeds give one result. All of its
# variation comes from the memoized `DirectedGraph.topologicalOrder`, which a `lazy val` cannot
# accept a generator for. A `(using Random)` would therefore have been a parameter that provably
# does nothing while reading as a guarantee of reproducibility -- the same false promise as the
# `Random(0)` the Java facade pinned before 1.13.0. Its signature is unchanged, so it appears
# nowhere in this file. See #158.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #153: Traversable.filteredAdjacentVertices, and its two implementors.
#
# The odd one out of its own family: `filteredAdjacencies` is the same shape for the same purpose
# and always took the generator. Invisible because nothing in src/main called it and its two
# tests ended in `.toSet`, which discards the only thing that varied.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.filteredAdjacentVertices(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.filteredAdjacentVertices(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.filteredAdjacentVertices(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #131: TopologicalSort.sort and traversal.
#
# A DAG generally admits many topological orders and adjacency order picks between them, so this
# is where the defect had the most visible effect and the least detectable one: no test can fail
# because of it, since every answer satisfies the postcondition.
#
# `DirectedGraph.topologicalOrder` is NOT in this list and does not change. It is a `lazy val`,
# a `val` takes no `using` clause, and so it remains unseedable by construction -- memoized,
# stable within a run, arbitrary across runs. That is the right trade for `isCyclic` and the
# Java facade, which want a stable answer rather than a pinned one, and it is why
# AcyclicShortestPaths stopped reading it (below).
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TopologicalSort.sort(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph)scala.Option")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TopologicalSort.traversal(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph)scala.Option")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #154: BellmanFord.shortestPaths, and DirectedGraph.shortestPaths, which delegates to it.
#
# Found by a sweep #131 asked for, and the ones a stated rule had excused. Through 1.13.x the
# facade's scaladoc said relaxation-based algorithms sort or relax over all edges and so cannot
# be affected by adjacency order. True of the distances, false of the predecessors -- and it is
# the predecessors that are returned. Which of several equal-cost paths you are told about is
# exactly what varies, the same variation Dijkstra has.
#
# Bellman-Ford is queue-based, which is why a seed does real work here where it would not in
# AcyclicShortestPaths: adjacency order decides the order vertices are enqueued and so dequeued,
# and a vertex may be relaxed several times, so the order propagates globally rather than staying
# within one vertex's edge list.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BellmanFord.shortestPaths(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph,java.lang.Object,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering)scala.Option")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph.shortestPaths(java.lang.Object,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering)scala.Option")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT listed here, because it is an addition rather than a break: the Java facade's
# `ShortestPaths.bellmanFord` gained a `java.util.Random` overload, bringing it into line with
# the ten pairs added in 1.13.0. A Java caller has no way to write a `given`, so where the order
# reaches the result the method comes in a pair. `ShortestPaths.acyclic` deliberately did not --
# see the note above.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #160: the four deprecated aliases are removed.
#
# None of them had a call site anywhere in this repository, and two had been deprecated for
# seven releases. A deprecated alias costs nothing to keep for one release and something to
# keep forever: it is published API, it appears in Scaladoc, and the next person reading
# `Traversable` meets two names for the vertex count and has to work out which is current.
#
# `util.GraphException` (since 1.7.0) existed because Gryphon once had two identical but
# unrelated `GraphException` case classes, so catching one did not catch the other. `core` has
# been the only one since. The whole file is gone; the rest of the `util` package stays.
#
# `N` and `M` (since 1.9.0) were replaced by `order` and `size`, the standard graph-theory terms
# and what the Java facade has always called them. MiMa reports five removals for two methods
# because the inherited copies on `AbstractGraph`, `AbstractAdjunctGraph`, `EdgeTraversable` and
# `VertexMap` are separate entries in the erased API.
#
# `DiscoverableVertex` had no `since` version, so it had given no formal notice. It goes anyway:
# it aliased a type declared in the same file. Removing it removes the synthetic `Vertex$package`
# holder with it, because that alias was the only top-level definition in Vertex.scala -- which
# is why two MissingClassProblem entries appear for what reads as one deletion, and the same for
# `GraphException$package`.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.N()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.N()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.N()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeTraversable.M()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.AbstractAdjunctGraph.M()Int")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Vertex$package")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Vertex$package$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.util.GraphException$package")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.util.GraphException$package$")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #157: ConnectedComponents.components returns a ConnectedResult case class, not a tuple.
#
# Until 1.14.0 `ConnectedResult[V, E]` was a type alias for `(Connexions[V, E], Map[V, Int])`:
# half first-class, since Connexions is itself a TraversalResult, and half a bare Map. The bare
# half was the one every caller used, so connected components was the one algorithm whose result
# could not be handed to a function written over "a vertex-keyed traversal result".
#
# It is now a case class extending AbstractVertexTraversalResult[V, Int], matching TopologicalSort,
# which has been that shape all along and is also a vertex-to-Int map. It gains `partition:
# Set[Set[V]]`, derived from the id map rather than stored so the two cannot drift -- the one view
# whose invariance needs no footnote, since it carries no arbitrary labelling.
#
# Source-incompatible as well as binary-incompatible: `val (_, m) = components(g)` stops
# compiling. Migration is mechanical -- `.map` for the ids, `.connexions` for the tree,
# `.partition` for the components.
#
# The two MissingClassProblem entries are the synthetic `ConnectedComponents$package` holder, which
# existed to carry the top-level type alias and goes with it, exactly as for `Vertex$package` in
# #160 above.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.ConnectedComponents.components(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable,scala.util.Random)scala.Tuple2")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.ConnectedComponents$package")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.ConnectedComponents$package$")
